 .../language/resource.language.en_gb/strings.po           | 4 ++--
 .../language/resource.language.es_ar/strings.po           | 8 ++++----
 .../language/resource.language.es_es/strings.po           | 8 ++++----
 .../language/resource.language.es_mx/strings.po           | 8 ++++----
 5 files changed, 16 insertions(+), 16 deletions(-)

diff --git a/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po b/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
index 5c5a903..29fcfe1 100644
--- a/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
+++ b/addons/resource.language.en_gb/resources/strings.po
@@ -27904,7 +27904,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 #. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadeshdmivrr"; translated in Estuary (#54141)
 msgctxt "#62141"
-msgid "Kodi does not change the refresh rate from one frame to the next, except for \"Video at its own rate with VRR\", so otherwise it has no use for VRR (G-SYNC, FreeSync), and some TVs go to their game mode while VRR is on. NVIDIA graphics tell an HDMI TV that declares VRR that it is on, in the modes from 50 Hz up that its VRR range covers, unless Kodi asks them not to. Off (the default): Kodi asks the driver to leave VRR off in every change of mode, so the TV is told that it is off; the picture Kodi sends does not change. On: the driver decides. \"Video at its own rate with VRR\" turns it on by itself while it is on. Dolby Vision always goes out with VRR off. Applies from the next change of mode, for example when the next video starts and changes the refresh rate."
+msgid "Kodi does not change the refresh rate from one frame to the next, except for \"Video at its own rate with VRR\", so otherwise it has no use for VRR (G-SYNC, FreeSync), and some TVs go to their game mode while VRR is on. NVIDIA graphics tell an HDMI TV that declares VRR that it is on, in the modes from 50 Hz up that its VRR range covers, unless Kodi asks them not to. Off (the default): Kodi asks the driver to leave VRR off in every change of mode, so the TV is told that it is off; the picture Kodi sends does not change. On: the driver decides. \"Video at its own rate with VRR\" turns it on by itself while it is on, and it is left on in the modes of the menus of a display that declares VRR and has no mode at the rate of films (23.976 Hz or a whole multiple of it) at their resolution, so that a film can play at its own rate there. Dolby Vision always goes out with VRR off. Applies from the next change of mode, for example when the next video starts and changes the refresh rate."
 msgstr ""
 
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
@@ -27916,7 +27916,7 @@ msgstr ""
 #: system/settings/settings.xml
 #. Hades: help of setting "videoscreen.hadesvrrvideo"; translated in Estuary (#54143)
 msgctxt "#62143"
-msgid "Plays a video at its own frame rate with the TV's variable refresh rate (VRR), in the mode of the menus: the screen does not go black when a video starts or stops. Each picture is shown at its own time; where the frame rate is under the lowest rate of the TV's VRR, each picture is shown two or more times. Only SDR and HDR10 videos: Dolby Vision, HDR10+, HLG, 3D and any video that this cannot show exactly change the mode as before. Needs \"Adjust display refresh rate\", and VRR turned on in the TV itself (on an LG TV: VRR & G-Sync for NVIDIA graphics, AMD FreeSync Premium for AMD graphics): the TV does not say whether that setting is on, so LibreELEC Hades cannot see it. On NVIDIA, \"HDMI variable refresh rate (VRR)\" must be on. In the first second of each video LibreELEC Hades checks that the refresh of the output follows the video; where it does not, the mode changes to the video's rate as before, and the player information says so (System page, VRR). kodi.log says what was chosen for each video and how exact its pictures were. Not yet seen working on a TV. Off by default."
+msgid "Plays a video at its own frame rate with the TV's variable refresh rate (VRR), in the mode of the menus: the screen does not go black when a video starts or stops. Each picture is shown at its own time; where the frame rate is under the lowest rate of the TV's VRR, each picture is shown two or more times. SDR and HDR10 videos, and HDR10+ where the graphics driver sends it next to VRR (NVIDIA RTX 40 and RTX 50 series): Dolby Vision, HLG, 3D, HDR10+ on other graphics and any video that this cannot show exactly change the mode as before. Needs \"Adjust display refresh rate\", and VRR turned on in the TV itself (on an LG TV: VRR & G-Sync for NVIDIA graphics, AMD FreeSync Premium for AMD graphics): the TV does not say whether that setting is on, so LibreELEC Hades cannot see it. On NVIDIA it turns \"HDMI variable refresh rate (VRR)\" on by itself. In the first second of each video LibreELEC Hades checks that the refresh of the output follows the video; where it does not, the mode changes to the video's rate as before, and the player information says so (System page, VRR). On a display that declares VRR and has no mode at the video's rate, a video plays this way also with this setting off. kodi.log says what was chosen for each video and how exact its pictures were. Not yet seen working on a TV. Off by default."
 msgstr ""
 
